---
export const title = 'Usage';
export const order = 100;
import DemoComponentCard from '../../../../components/DemoComponentCard.astro';
---

<p>
  <code>wc-typing-indicator</code> shows that a reply is on its way: three pulsing dots and a short
  status. Say what is happening rather than only "Thinking…", and change <code>label</code> as the
  work moves on.
</p>

<DemoComponentCard lang={'html'}>
  <div style='display: flex; flex-direction: column; gap: 1rem;'>
    <wc-typing-indicator></wc-typing-indicator>
    <wc-typing-indicator id='typing-steps' label='Looking up your laptop in Asset management…'></wc-typing-indicator>
  </div>
  <script is:inline>
    (function () {
      var steps = [
        'Looking up your laptop in Asset management…',
        'Checking your leave balance in Human resources…',
        'Writing the drafts…',
      ];
      var index = 0;
      var el = document.querySelector('#typing-steps');
      setInterval(function () {
        index = (index + 1) % steps.length;
        el.label = steps[index];
      }, 2500);
    })();
  </script>
</DemoComponentCard>

<h3>People typing</h3>
<p>
  The same indicator shows people typing in a discussion. Put their avatar in the <code>start</code>
  slot. It does not depend on the page's AI settings.
</p>

<DemoComponentCard lang={'html'}>
  <wc-typing-indicator label='Ravi is typing'>
    <wc-avatar slot='start' name='Ravi Kumar' size='sm'></wc-avatar>
  </wc-typing-indicator>
</DemoComponentCard>

<h3>Accessibility</h3>
<p>
  The indicator is a polite status region (<code>role="status"</code>): screen readers read the label
  when it appears and each time it changes, never in a loop. The dots are hidden from them, and with
  reduced motion they hold still. The default label is the <code>typingThinking</code> string.
</p>
